   "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
   "text": "The specific sequence 'PNR fail then PULL_UP' might not occur frequently enough for every unit (team/lineup) to provide sufficient data for robust statistical analysis, especially when requiring '>=70% of units' to show lift. This could lead to low power for many individual unit tests."
  },
  {
   "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
   "text": "Testing for 'lift > 0' in '>=70% of units' implies performing multiple hypothesis tests (one for each unit). Without appropriate multiplicity control, the probability of false positives increases significantly, potentially leading to an overestimation of units showing 'lift'."
  },
  {
   "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
   "text": "The reliability of identifying 'PNR' and 'PULL_UP' play types from the derived possession records, especially in sequential contexts, is critical. Historical evidence (FAM:SF-APPS-012) flagged potential 'tagging differences' for PNR share, suggesting this is a known risk."
  },
  {
   "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
   "text": "Basketball strategies and defensive schemes evolve. Analyzing data across four seasons (2022-23 to 2025-26) might introduce non-stationarity if the effectiveness of PNR or PULL_UP shots, or the definition of 'PNR fail', changes over time."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.3,
  "rejected": 0.2,
  "untestable": 0.2,
  "artifact": 0.1,
  "uncertain": 0.2
 },
 "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The average Points Per Possession (PPP) of PULL_UP shots that immediately follow a 'PNR fail' sequence within the same possession, compared to the baseline PPP of PULL_UP shots that do not follow a 'PNR fail', aggregated across NBA teams (units) from the 2022-23 to 2025-26 seasons.",
  "unit": "Offensive team-season. Each NBA team in each season (2022-23 to 2025-26) will be considered a 'unit'.",
  "sample": "All possessions from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25 regular seasons and playoffs, and NBA 2025-26 regular season and playoffs (as available). 'PNR fail' is defined as a possession where a PNR play type is initiated, but the possession ends without a shot attempt by the PNR initiator or roller, and is immediately followed by a PULL_UP shot by any player on the same offensive team. The baseline for comparison will be all PULL_UP shots not immediately preceded by a 'PNR fail' within the same possession. Only units with a minimum of 50 'PNR fail -> PULL_UP' sequences and 200 baseline PULL_UP shots will be included in the analysis to ensure sufficient sample size for individual unit lift calculation.",
  "power": "A two-sample t-test will be used for each unit to determine if the PPP of 'PNR fail -> PULL_UP' sequences is significantly greater than the baseline PULL_UP PPP. Power calculations will target detecting a 2pp lift with 80% power at alpha=0.05 for each unit. For the league-wide assessment, the target of >=70% of units showing lift > 0 and median lift > 2pp will be assessed based on the distribution of individual unit results.",
  "controls": "To control for confounding factors, two primary approaches will be employed: 1) Matching: Attempt to match 'PNR fail -> PULL_UP' sequences with baseline PULL_UP shots on variables such as shot clock remaining, quarter, score differential, and offensive player taking the shot. 2) Regression: A mixed-effects model with team and season random effects, controlling for covariates like shot clock, game state, and player identity, to estimate the marginal effect of 'PNR fail' on subsequent PULL_UP PPP. The baseline PULL_UP PPP will be defined as PULL_UP shots that are *not* immediately preceded by a PNR play type in the same possession, to isolate the PNR-failure-contingent effect.",
  "falsification": "An alternative hypothesis of 'PNR fail -> DRIVE' or 'PNR fail -> SPOT_UP' showing similar or greater lift would falsify the specific claim that PULL_UP is *the* counter. Also, if PULL_UP PPP *decreases* after PNR fail, it would falsify the hypothesis.",
  "multiplicity": "For the '>=70% of units' criterion, a False Discovery Rate (FDR) control method (e.g., Benjamini-Hochberg) will be applied to the p-values from the individual unit t-tests to account for multiple comparisons when determining the percentage of units with statistically significant lift > 0. The median lift > 2pp will be assessed on the point estimates of lift for all included units, irrespective of individual significance, to address the magnitude criterion.",
  "decision_rule": "The hypothesis will be considered 'supported' if, after FDR correction for individual unit tests, at least 70% of included units show a statistically significant lift (p<alpha_FDR) in PPP for 'PNR fail -> PULL_UP' sequences compared to baseline PULL_UP, AND the median lift across all included units is greater than 2.0 points per possession. Otherwise, the hypothesis is 'rejected' (if sufficient power) or 'uncertain'.",
  "data_version": "Derived play-type possession records from NBA 2022-23, 2023-24, 2024-25, 2025-26 regular seasons and playoffs, including play-by-play data, substitution intervals, starters, defensive matchup minutes, and box-score summaries. Data will be finalized as of 2026-06-21 for the 2025-26 playoffs."
 }
}

